```cpp
template<int N>
simdjson_warn_unused simdjson_really_inline bool json_iterator::copy_to_buffer(const uint8_t *json, uint8_t (&tmpbuf)[N]) noexcept {
  // Truncate whitespace to fit the buffer.
  auto len = peek_length(-1);
  if (len > N-1) {
    if (jsoncharutils::is_not_structural_or_whitespace(json[N])) { return false; }
    len = N-1;
  }
  ...
}
```

This reads `json[N]`. For `parse_root_bool`, `N = 6`. So it reads `json[6]`. If `json` points near the end of the buffer, `json[6]` could be out of bounds.

But `json` points to a token in the JSON document, which is within the actual data. So `json[6]` would be within the padding.

Hmm, let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is that `json` points to the very end of the actual data. For example, if the JSON document is `true` (4 bytes), then `json` points to position 0, and `json[6]` would be at position 6, which is within the padding (positions 4-35).

But what if the JSON document is just `t` (1 byte)? Then `json` points to position 0, and `json[6]` would be at position 6, which is within the padding (positions 1-32).

So the padding should prevent out-of-bounds reads.

